# Agent instructions for <%= app_name %>

## Running tests

- Unit and integration tests: `bin/rails test`

## Simple, straightforward implementation

Prefer implementation that junior developers can read, trace and maintain.

- Use clear names that match the business concepts in the design documents.
- Keep functions and methods small and focused on one understandable task.
- Follow familiar Rails and PostgreSQL conventions so readers know where to look.
- Make the flow from input, through validation and business rules, to the result
  easy to follow. Keep data changes and other side effects visible.
- Add an abstraction only when it solves a concrete, current need. Explain that
  need; avoid extra layers, generic frameworks or clever techniques for possible
  future requirements.
- Preserve the planned tenant boundaries, module behavior and business rules
  when simplifying code. Simplicity must not remove required checks.
- When complexity is unavoidable, explain why it is needed and how it works near
  the relevant code or in a linked design document. Use a small example when it
  helps a reader follow the behavior.
- Keep changes focused and explain their business purpose in plain language.

## Review question

Can a junior developer explain how the feature works, from input to result,
without the original author beside them? If not, simplify the implementation or
clarify the necessary complexity before considering the change ready.
